# Parting ways with a companion. ONE shared scene for all of them:
# opened by right-clicking a story companion (TSC_PartWays), with {NPC}
# carrying whichever name it is.
#
# The guards live in code (CanPartHere): no dismissing anyone mid-fight,
# underground, downed, or carrying a piece of the crown. By the time this
# opens, the parting is allowed - so the scene's only job is to make sure
# the player meant it, and to let them say why.
#
# {PLAYER} is whoever walked over to say it.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_PartWays

== start ==
{NPC} reads {PLAYER}'s face before a word is said. A season on the road does that to people.

"Ah," they say. "That kind of talk."

* "This is where we part ways." -> devoted
    if affinity(>=25)
* "This is where we part ways." -> why
    if affinity(<25)
* [Actually, never mind.] -> end

# Devoted companions do not simply take it. They are not refusing - they
# will go if the player says it twice - but somebody who has followed this
# company through everything gets to make them say it twice.
== devoted ==
Whatever they were braced for, it was not that.

"No," they say. Not angry. Just factual, the way you correct a date. "No, I do not think it is."

They do not move.

"I have been at your shoulder for every bad hour of this. I have buried people for you. If you are sending me off now, you can do me the courtesy of meaning it out loud, twice, while I am looking at you."

* "I mean it. This is where we part ways." -> why
* [Let it go. They stay.] -> stayed

== stayed ==
Something in them unclenches, and they have the grace not to say anything about it.

"Right," they say. "Then I will get the fire going."

The camp does not discuss it. The camp does, however, notice.

* [Get on with the evening.] -> end
    do affinity(+5)

== why ==
They do not argue. That is somehow worse than arguing.

"Say why. Not for the argument. I would just rather carry the true reason than spend a year guessing at it."

* "You have done enough. This last stretch does not need to be yours." -> spared
* "The company is going somewhere I would not ask anyone to follow." -> honest
* "It is simply time." -> blunt
* [Reconsider.] -> end

== spared ==
"Enough." {NPC} turns the word over. "That is a kinder way to put it than most would manage."

A nod, slowly.

"I will take it. It even has the advantage of being partly true."

* [Let them go.] -> depart
* [Ask them to stay after all.] -> end

== honest ==
"No. You would not." A short breath that is almost a laugh. "And you are right that I would follow anyway if you asked, which I suspect is the actual reason you are not asking."

They shoulder their pack.

"Fine. I have been the one left behind before. It is survivable."

* [Let them go.] -> depart
* [Ask them to stay after all.] -> end

== blunt ==
"It is," {NPC} agrees. "Roads end. Companies come apart. Anyone who tells you otherwise has not been on many."

They check their kit with the unhurried thoroughness of somebody who intends to be walking a long way.

"No hard feelings that I intend to mention."

* [Let them go.] -> depart
* [Ask them to stay after all.] -> end

== depart ==
There is a handshake, or something close enough to one.

"If it goes badly," {NPC} says, "I would rather hear it from a stranger in a tavern than not hear it at all. So: be somewhere a storyteller can find you."

And then they are walking, and not looking back, because looking back is for people who intend to return and they have not decided about that yet.

* [Watch them go.] -> end
    do part_ways()
